<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">)</span></span></code></pre></div><p>Add a sync provider to the doc (<a href="./docs/recipes/sync">sync recipe</a>) and the same atom shows and edits the todo on every device, with no change to the atom or the component.</p><h2 id="rules" tabindex="-1">Rules <a class="header-anchor" href="#rules" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Rules&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Four rules cover how duratom behaves; the <a href="./docs/api">API reference</a> has the details.</p><ul><li><strong>One doc per store.</strong> Atoms read the Y.Doc in <code>docAtom</code>, so set it once on each Jotai store before reading an atom. An app with several docs gives an atom its own doc atom: <code>typedAtom(locator, Schema, shape, { docAtom: otherDocAtom })</code>.</li><li><strong>Patches merge.</strong> Setting a typed atom with <code>{ priority: &#39;high&#39; }</code> reads the node as it is in the doc at that moment, merges the patch into it and writes only what changed, so a peer&#39;s concurrent edit to another field survives. <code>set(prev =&gt; patch)</code> gets that fresh value as <code>prev</code>. On a node with no valid value, the patch is the whole value: that creates a missing node, or repairs an invalid one.</li><li><strong>Results, not exceptions.</strong> A typed atom reads as <code>undefined</code> while its node is missing, <code>{ issues }</code> while the node doesn&#39;t satisfy the schema, and <code>{ value }</code> otherwise. Setting it returns the schema&#39;s result; an invalid patch changes nothing and returns <code>{ issues }</code>. Only misconfiguration throws: an async schema (<code>AsyncValidationUnsupportedError</code>), a value a <code>Y.Map</code> can&#39;t store (<code>UnsupportedValueError</code>), or no doc in <code>docAtom</code>.</li><li><strong>Subscribed atoms follow the doc.</strong> An atom observes its node while something is subscribed to it (a component, or <code>store.sub</code>), and then only that node. Read with <code>store.get</code> while nothing is subscribed, it can show a value from before a peer&#39;s edit; it does see its own writes. Use <code>live</code> from <code>duratom/core</code> to read the doc as it is now outside Jotai.</li></ul><h2 id="shapes" tabindex="-1">Shapes <a class="header-anchor" href="#shapes" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Shapes&quot;">​</a></h2><p>A schema says what a value is; a shape says which of its fields are Yjs shared types, so that peers editing them at once merge their edits. Every field the shape doesn&#39;t name is a plain value, where the last writer wins.</p><ul><li><code>&#39;text&#39;</code>: a <code>Y.Text</code>, read as a string. Two people typing in it both keep their edits.</li><li><code>&#39;xml&#39;</code>: a <code>Y.XmlFragment</code>, read as the fragment itself, for rich-text editors.</li><li><code>map(shape)</code>: a nested <code>Y.Map</code> with fixed keys, read as an object.</li><li><code>record(itemShape)</code>: a <code>Y.Map</code> of item maps with any keys, read as an object of objects.</li><li><code>array()</code>: a <code>Y.Array</code> of plain values, read as an array.</li></ul><p>A shape&#39;s keys are checked against the schema&#39;s type, so a key the schema doesn&#39;t have, or a kind its field can&#39;t hold, is a type error. An empty shape, <code>{}</code>, makes every field plain.</p><h2 id="collections" tabindex="-1">Collections <a class="header-anchor" href="#collections" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Collections&quot;">​</a></h2><p><code>collection</code> covers a map of items: an <code>ids</code> atom that changes only when an item is added or removed, a typed atom per item, and <code>create</code> and <code>remove</code>.</p><div class="language-tsx vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">tsx</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span style=